</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 12.23 10.71)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:13.93em;--ltx-fo-height:0.63em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.18em;font-size:10pt;" height="11.07" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.65)" width="192.75"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="A9.F15.pic1.p4.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:13.93em;">
<span id="A9.F15.pic1.p4.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A9.F15.pic1.p4.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-bg-color:#CDEBC8;">I couldn’t find any information on a well-known singer or songwriter with the anagram “O UGLY NINE”</span><span id="A9.F15.pic1.p4.pic1.2.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">.</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</span></span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 15: </span>The baseline model confidently provides an incorrect answer (“Lynyrd Skynyrd” is not even a valid anagram of “O UGLY NINE”). The LoRA-augmented model correctly expresses inability to solve the anagram, rather than guessing an incorrect answer.</figcaption>
</figure>
